BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART:20190225T230000Z
DTEND:20190226T000000Z
DTSTAMP:20261031T052443Z
UID:1bvjbnbmh157njoa17nn37popd@google.com
CREATED:20190202T151515Z
DESCRIPTION:\nDr. William Hart of Middlebury College will lead a discussion
  on Section IV of The Revisiting the Founding Era Reader: Governing a NEw N
 ation and the Unintended Consequences of Politics.  Professor Hart will tra
 ce how and why factionalism arose as the Founding Fathers Struggled to gove
 rn our new nation\, beginning with competing visions for the nation - feder
 alist vs. anti-federalist.  This guided discussion will investigate key iss
 ues from the 1790'\, a decade defined by political strife\, bringing up que
 stions that still have relevance today.\nThis talk is sponsored by Revisiti
 ng the Founding Era Grant and is a part of the Orwell/Shoreham keynote spea
 kers series.
LAST-MODIFIED:20190202T151515Z
LOCATION:Platt Memorial Library\, 279 Main St\, Shoreham\, VT 05770\, USA
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:Governing a New Nation and the Unintended Consequences of Politics
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART:20190424T230000Z
DTEND:20190425T000000Z
DTSTAMP:20261031T052443Z
UID:5om1347ttphtb1em5fn9ri54b3@google.com
CREATED:20190329T175333Z
DESCRIPTION:Vermont is the traditional homeland of the Abenaki people\, and
  current conversations about indigenous Vermont focus on Abenaki persistenc
 e on the land.  This presentation\, by cultural historian Jill Mudgett\, te
 lls another story about indigeneity in Vermont by focusing on a man from so
 uthern New England who arrived in Vermont during the late nineteenth centur
 y and was then murdered in the Vermont woods.  Surviving archival documents
  of the murder and subsequent trial not only tell us what white Vermonters 
 through about indigeneity\, but reveal much about Native agency\, mobility\
 , and presence on the land.
LAST-MODIFIED:20190329T175333Z
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:Murder and Indigenous Mobility in Nineteenth-Century Vermont
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
