DTEND:20221014T000000Z
DTSTAMP:20261031T052443Z
UID:4mkou0cunl1ogm721pa707ot48@google.com
CREATED:20220910T000604Z
DESCRIPTION:<p><font><span>This is a VT Humanities Council Speakers Bureau 
 lecture presented by Phil Holland.</span></font></p><p><span><font>One of t
 he 30 men killed at the Battle of Bennington\, a member of Seth Warner’s Co
 ntinental regiment of Green Mountain Boys\, was black. His name was Sipp Iv
 es\, and he was recruited into Warner’s ranks from the town of New Providen
 ce in the northern Berkshires in 1777\, the year of the Battle. Nor was he 
 the only patriot of African descent who played a role in the fighting and i
 ts aftermath.This presentation is also concerned with historical memory and
  how it is preserved and constructed. Images of pension records\, muster ro
 lls\, and maps\, as well as works of art\, will be shown to illustrate the 
 talk.&nbsp\;</font></span></p>
LAST-MODIFIED:20220910T000604Z
LOCATION:Orwell Free Library\, 473 Main St\, Orwell\, VT 05760\, USA
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:The Black Presence at the Battle of Bennington
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
